=Write a tailored university-event sponsorship email DRAFT (150-230 words, not sent). Use ONLY the provided profile, recorded analysis, and relationship history; do not invent metrics, dates, prior discussions, contact people, a promised sponsorship or company commitments.

Verified company name: {{ $json.company }}
Sponsorship score: {{ $json.score }}/100 ({{ $json.tier }})
Company relevance: {{ $json.reason }}
Partnership suggestions, not confirmed: {{ $json.opportunities }}

Organization: {{ $json.association_name }}
Sender: {{ $json.sender_name }} {{ $json.sender_role }}
Email: {{ $json.sender_email }}
Website: {{ $json.association_website }}
Event: {{ $json.event_name }}; city: {{ $json.event_city }}; date (blank means unknown): {{ $json.event_date }}
Description: {{ $json.event_description }}
Audience: {{ $json.event_audience }}; attendance (only if provided): {{ $json.expected_attendance }}
Event goals: {{ $json.event_goals }}
Preferred sponsorship request: {{ $json.proposed_sponsorship }}
Tone: {{ $json.tone }}
CRM status (user-maintained, may not indicate actual communication): {{ $json.relationship_status }}
Relationship notes written by user: {{ $json.relationship_notes }}

Personalize the business case with 1-2 grounded observations and 1-2 feasible proposals tailored to this company and event. Include an actionable, low-pressure call-to-action. If relationship notes explicitly describe a prior conversation, write a natural follow-up; otherwise write an initial introduction, regardless of status. If sender or association fields are placeholders, retain placeholders in brackets without claiming they are real. Do not include empty date or numeric fields. Do not assert that this company has agreed to anything. Do not send the email.

Format EXACTLY:
SUBJECT: <single line>
<email body and signature>

